Final advanced project: a **notes app** that persists between reloads. It combines everything — the event loop, functions, arrays, and the localStorage pattern. The golden rule from the last lesson: **save on change, load on start**.

### The plan
1. On load, read notes from localStorage (default `[]`)
2. Render each note as an `<li>` inside `#note-list`
3. On form submit, push the new note to the array
4. Save the array with `JSON.stringify` and re-render

### Loading notes on start
```js
let notes = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("notes")) || [];
```
The `|| []` guard matters — if nothing is stored yet, `getItem` returns `null`, and we want an empty array.

### Rendering the list
```js
function render() {
  list.innerHTML = "";
  notes.forEach((note) => {
    const li = document.createElement("li");
    li.textContent = note;
    list.appendChild(li);
  });
}
```
A small render function that wipes and rebuilds the list from the array keeps the UI in sync with state — the same idea behind React and Vue.

### Saving on change
```js
function save() {
  localStorage.setItem("notes", JSON.stringify(notes));
}
```
Call `save()` every time the array changes, then `render()`.

### Adding and deleting
```js
form.addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  const value = input.value.trim();
  if (!value) return;
  notes.push(value);
  input.value = "";
  save();
  render();
});
```
For delete, render a button per note and remove by index: `notes.splice(index, 1)` then save and render again.

Every feature follows the same loop: mutate the array → save → re-render. Master that rhythm and you can build any CRUD app.

